course: dictionary
lesson: 11862
title: Informal words in everyday talk
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to everyday talk, not to school books. Bonkers is one of them. People use it with friends and family, and you hear it mostly in the UK and Australia. Informal words are fine in chatty writing and fun stories, but you would not use them in a formal letter.
explain: Bonkers has two meanings. The first is crazy, silly or mad. «That idea is bonkers!» means the idea is so silly that it seems mad. The second is very excited or angry, as in «go bonkers». «The dog went bonkers when we came home» shows the dog was wildly excited, bouncing and barking.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. If the sentence is playful, silly or surprised, bonkers probably means crazy. If someone is bouncing with joy or shouting with anger, it probably means excited or angry. Then swap in your meaning and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Bonkers is an informal word, mainly used in the UK and Australia.
fact: «Bonkers» means crazy, silly or mad.
fact: «Go bonkers» means to become very excited or angry.
fact: «Stark raving bonkers» and «totally bonkers» are stronger ways of saying something is very silly or mad.
fact: Bonkers is a mild, playful word, so it is not rude, but it is still informal.
exercise: «That idea is bonkers!» What does bonkers mean here? | Quiet and calm | *Crazy or silly | Wet and muddy | Very clever and useful
exercise: «The dog went bonkers when we came home.» What does went bonkers mean? | *Became very excited | Fell fast asleep | Ran out of the house | Became very quiet and still
exercise: Dad says, «You are totally bonkers to swim in this cold sea!» What does totally bonkers mean? | Very slow | Very cold and wet | Completely dry | *Completely crazy
test: «Mum went bonkers when she saw the mess.» What does went bonkers suggest? | Was very tired | Was very calm and smiling | Fell asleep | *Was very angry
test: Ella says, «Flying pigs? That's bonkers!» What does bonkers mean in her sentence? | Very sleepy | Very tasty | *Crazy or silly | Very fast
exam: Which meaning does bonkers have in «The crowd went bonkers when the goal went in»? | *Very excited | Hard to see | Crazy or silly | Very old
